import Article from "../../../layouts/Article.astro";
import KeyTakeaway from "../../../components/KeyTakeaway.astro";
import InfoBox from "../../../components/InfoBox.astro";
import StepList from "../../../components/StepList.astro";
import Step from "../../../components/Step.astro";
import QuickFact from "../../../components/QuickFact.astro";
---

<Article
  title="What Is Scythe: Duel of Meloch? The 2026 Release Explained"
  description="Stonemaier's two-player duel between Scythe and Expeditions — how it works and what's in it."
  game="duel-of-meloch"
  gameTitle="Duel of Meloch"
  category="Guide"
  product="scythe-duel-of-meloch-insert"
  publishDate="2026-08-09"
>
  <p>
    Stonemaier Games released <strong>Scythe: Duel of Meloch</strong> in July 2026,
    and it's an unusual thing: a standalone two-player game that puts Scythe on one
    side of the table and Expeditions on the other. If you've been trying to work
    out whether it's an expansion, a spin-off or a new game entirely, the answer is
    closest to "all three".
  </p>

  <KeyTakeaway answer="A standalone ~45-minute duel where one player commands a Scythe faction and the other an Expeditions crew, racing to place four stars on a shared Triumph Track. It also contains a brand-new Scythe faction and the eighth player mat." />

  <div class="my-8 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
    <QuickFact label="Players" value="2" icon="👥" />
    <QuickFact label="Time" value="~45 min" icon="⏱️" />
    <QuickFact label="Released" value="July 2026" icon="📅" />
    <QuickFact label="Standalone" value="Yes" icon="📦" />
  </div>

  <h2>Two Games, One Table</h2>

  <p>
    The central idea is asymmetry taken further than Scythe has gone before. It's
    not two factions facing off — it's two <em>games</em> facing off.
  </p>

  <div class="my-6 grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
    <div class="p-5 bg-white rounded-xl border border-bark/10">
      <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-bark mb-2">⚙️ The Scythe side</h4>
      <p class="text-sm text-bark/60">One player commands a Scythe faction with Scythe rules and components — mechs, workers, the familiar action-mat economy. Any faction works, including the new one in the box.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="p-5 bg-white rounded-xl border border-bark/10">
      <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-bark mb-2">🧭 The Expeditions side</h4>
      <p class="text-sm text-bark/60">The other player leads an Expeditions crew, playing by that game's logic instead. Same world, same era, entirely different toolkit.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    Both sides are chasing the same objective, which is what makes the asymmetry
    work rather than just feel lopsided.
  </p>

  <h2>How a Game Plays Out</h2>

  <StepList title="The shape of a duel">
    <Step number={1} title="A compact map">
      Play happens on a small double-sided board with just ten locations and two
      lakes. After Scythe's sprawling map, the tightness is the point — there's
      nowhere to hide and no long build-up before you're in contact.
    </Step>
    <Step number={2} title="Race the Triumph Track">
      Both players are placing stars on a single shared Triumph Track. The first
      to place <strong>four stars</strong> wins.
    </Step>
    <Step number={3} title="Every star helps your opponent">
      Whenever you place a star, your opponent draws a <strong>Meloch card</strong>
      and picks a reward from it. Pulling ahead actively arms the player behind
      you.
    </Step>
    <Step number={4} title="Finish in about 45 minutes">
      The short track and the catch-up pressure keep games tight and quick — a
      fraction of a full game of Scythe.
    </Step>
  </StepList>

  <InfoBox type="rule" title="The Meloch cards are the design's clever bit">
    A four-star race could easily snowball, where the first player to score gets
    the resources to score again. Handing your opponent a reward every time you
    advance is a rubber band built into the win condition itself, and it's why
    finishes stay close.
  </InfoBox>

  <h2>What Else Is in the Box</h2>

  <p>
    For existing Scythe owners, this is arguably the headline. Duel of Meloch
    contains:
  </p>

  <div class="my-6 space-y-3">
    <div class="p-4 bg-white rounded-xl border border-bark/10">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-1">
        <span class="px-2 py-0.5 bg-purple-100 text-purple-700 text-[11px] font-bold rounded-full">NEW FACTION</span>
        <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-bark">Constantinople</h4>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-bark/60">The first brand-new Scythe faction in years. It plays in light gray — distinct from Polania's white — and does something no other faction does: it uses <strong>stars as a resource</strong>.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="p-4 bg-white rounded-xl border border-bark/10">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-1">
        <span class="px-2 py-0.5 bg-purple-100 text-purple-700 text-[11px] font-bold rounded-full">NEW MAT</span>
        <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-bark">Adventurous — the eighth player mat</h4>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-bark/60">The last remaining configuration of top and bottom actions not found on the other seven mats. For a game where the faction/mat pairing matters more than the faction alone, an eighth mat meaningfully widens the combination space.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <InfoBox type="tip" title="It works in both directions">
    All your existing Scythe factions, player mats, encounter cards and factory
    cards work inside Duel of Meloch — and Constantinople plus the Adventurous mat
    drop straight into your normal 1–7 player games of Scythe. If you already own
    Scythe, this box effectively doubles as a faction expansion. There's a
    <a href="/games/duel-of-meloch/constantinople-faction-guide/">full guide to Constantinople here</a>.
  </InfoBox>

  <h2>Who Is It For?</h2>

  <p>
    Three groups, in rough order of how obvious the buy is:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Scythe owners who mostly play two-player.</strong> Scythe at two is fine but never quite what it wants to be. A purpose-built duel at 45 minutes solves a real problem, and you get a faction and a mat out of it.</li>
    <li><strong>Couples and pairs who want a weeknight game.</strong> The whole appeal is that it fits in an evening rather than consuming one.</li>
    <li><strong>People new to the universe.</strong> It's standalone, so you can start here — though you'll be learning two asymmetric systems at once, which is a steeper first lesson than a normal game.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    For how it feels next to the original, see
    <a href="/games/duel-of-meloch/duel-of-meloch-vs-scythe/">Duel of Meloch vs Scythe</a>.
    For the buying decision, see
    <a href="/games/duel-of-meloch/is-duel-of-meloch-worth-it/">is it worth it</a>.
  </p>

  <InfoBox type="warning" title="It's new — treat rankings with caution">
    Duel of Meloch has only been out since July 2026. Constantinople's real
    strength in regular Scythe, and the Adventurous mat's place in the mat
    hierarchy, will take the community months of play to settle. Anyone
    confidently tier-listing either right now is guessing.
  </InfoBox>
</Article>
